-SED is a commands for editing, transforming, filtering, and extracting text, especially when working with files, logs, configuration files, and command output.
 Stream editor (SED) Unlike a normal text editor such as nano or vim, sed usually processes text line by line without opening an interactive editor.
Syntax: sed [OPTIONS] 'COMMAND' [FILE]
sed 's/old/new/' file.txt # 's = substitute #Read file.txt, replace the first occurrence of old with new on each line, and print the result.
if file.txt: hello old takers, my old self \n leave old people alone, you old man . Output: hello new takers, my old self  \n leave new people alone.
NB: By default, sed does not modify the original file.
Substitution Flags
Flag           	Meaning
global g	   Replace all matches on the line (not just first) *sed 's/foo/bar/g' file.txt 
N (number)	 Replace only the Nth match *sed 's/foo/bar/2' file.txt #second match and not the first one
Ng	         Replace from the Nth match onward
p	           Print the line if a substitution was made
w file	     Write the line to file if substitution made
I or i	     Case-insensitive matching (GNU) *sed 's/foo/bar/gI' file.txt # all occurence & Case-insensitive
m or M	     Multi-line mode (GNU)
e	           Execute result as a shell command (GNU)

Command-Line Options (Flags)
Flag	Description
-n	Suppress automatic printing (use with p)
-e 'script'	Add script (use multiple times for multiple scripts)
-f file	Read script from file
-i[SUFFIX]	Edit in place; optional backup suffix (-i.bak)
-E / -r	Use extended regular expressions (ERE)
-s	Treat files as separate streams (not concatenated)
-z	Separate lines by NUL instead of newline
--help	Show help
--version	Show version

Deleting Lines with 'd 
sed '5d' file.txt     # Delete line 5
sed '2,4d' file.txt   # Delete lines 2–4
sed '/^#/d' file.txt  # Delete comments
sed '/^$/d' file.txt  # Delete blank lines
sed '/^$/d'; '/^#/d' file.txt # deletes both blank and commented lines 
sed '$d' file.txt  # Deletes the last line
sed '/pattern/!d' file.txt  # Keep only matching lines
sed 's/^[[:space:]]*//' file.txt # removes leading blank spaces
sed 's/[[:space:]]*$//' file.txt #removes trailing blank spaces
sed 's/\t/    /g' file.txt # replaces spaces/tab

Printing (with -n) # -n: Suppresses default printing. By default, sed prints every line of the file. -n tells sed to print nothing unless explicitly requested.
sed -n '5p' file.txt              # Print only line 5
sed -n '$p' file.txt              # Print the last line
sed -n '10,20p' file.txt          # Print lines 10–20
sed -n '/errorsss/p' log.txt      # Print matching lines (like grep)
sed -n '/errorsss/Ip' log.txt     #case-insensitivity  
sed -n '/^ERRORSS/p' logfile #will return lines starting with ERRORSS e.g ERRORSS connection refused #but not : Warnings ERRORSS
sed -n '/failed$/p' logfile #will return lines ending with failed e.g database failed # but not: failed database
sed -n '/ibere/,/ipari/p' file.txt  # finds ibere and ipari, returns whats in between them

Modifying with sed
N.B with -i, There is no output to inspect first, and the original file is changed. so run code without -i first to be sure of the output.
sed -i 's/foo/bar/g' file.txt         # Edit file directly
-i.bak #this is used create a backup
sed -i.bak 's/foo/bar/g' file.txt     # Edit + backup to file.txt.bak
sed -i -e 's/a/b/' -e 's/c/d/' f.txt  # Multiple edits in place

Insert / Append / Change
sed '3i\this is a NEW LINE' file.txt    # Insert text into a file using inserting a text into a particular line 
sed '3a\NEW LINE' file.txt        # Append after line 3
sed '3c\REPLACED' file.txt        # Replace (change) line 3
sed '/header/a\---' file.txt      # Append after matches

# Multiple operations can be performed using -e 
sed -e 's/foo/bar/g' -e 's/baz/qux/' file.txt #will make multiple changes at once
sed 's/foo/bar/g; s/test/demo/g' file.txt #using ; also works

SED CAN BE PUT AND USED AS A FILE
*echo "s/foo/bar/g; #no need to actually put sed in the commands
s/test/demo/g;
/^#/d" > command.sed
--- then
* sed -f commands.sed input.txt #good for automation

Addressing specific lines
You can tell sed exactly which lines an operation should affect.
- Line number
 * sed '5s/foo/bar/' file.txt  #Only line 5 is affected.
- Range
 * sed '5,10s/foo/bar/' file.txt # Only lines 5–10.
- Pattern
 * sed '/ERROR/s/foo/bar/' logfile # Only lines containing ERROR are modified.
- Range between patterns
* sed '/BEGIN/,/END/d' file.txt # Deletes everything in between these two, This is extremely useful for configuration files and generated text.

OTHER COMMANDS
* sed 's/^\(.*\) \(.*\)$/\2, \1/' names.txt # turns Name Surname -> Name, Surname
* sed -E 's/(\w+)@(\w+)\.com/\2.\1/' emails.txt # Using capture groups
* sed 's/.*/\U&/' file.txt # Uppercase whole line
* sed -E 's#([0-9]{2})/([0-9]{2})/([0-9]{4})#\3-\1-\2#' dates.txt # Reformat dates MM/DD/YYYY → YYYY-MM-DD
* sed -nE 's/.*([0-9]{1,3}(\.[0-9]{1,3}){3}).*/\1/p' file.txt # Extract IP address

Key Concepts to Remember
- Pattern space — the current line being processed.
- Cycle — read line → execute commands → auto-print → next line.
- -n + p is the classic idiom for "print only what I say."
- -i is destructive; always test without it first or use a backup suffix.
- Delimiters in s can be any character (s#a#b# is useful when / is in the pattern).








